    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rental Cost</span>
    <h1>Honda Wave Rental Price in Hanoi</h1>
    <p>The Honda Wave is the most common motorbike on Vietnamese roads, and it is also the cheapest bike category in our published rental list. Riders choose it because it is light, simple and cheap to run, which matters when you are riding through Hanoi traffic every day of your trip. This guide covers the verified Honda Wave rental price in Hanoi for every rental period, how the rates compare with the other models we rent, and how to decide whether the Wave is the right bike for you.</p>
    <p>Every figure below comes from our published <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, which lists verified daily, weekly and monthly rates for the whole fleet. Nothing on this page is an estimate, a promotion or a discounted teaser rate.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Why the Honda Wave is so popular in Vietnam</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Honda Wave rental prices in Hanoi</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">How daily, weekly and monthly rates compare</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Honda Wave versus other rental bikes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">What riding a semi-automatic actually feels like</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">Everyday running: fuel, storage and trips</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">Who should rent a Honda Wave</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-8">How to book a Honda Wave</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the Honda Wave is so popular in Vietnam</h2>
    <p>The Wave has been sold in Vietnam for decades, and you will see it everywhere: delivery riders, families taking children to school, shop owners moving stock, and farmers carrying produce from the countryside. That popularity has practical consequences for renters. Spare parts are easy to find, every mechanic in the country knows the bike, and the riding position is familiar to anyone who has spent time in Vietnam before.</p>
    <p>The Wave is also light. A lighter bike is easier to push, park and pick up, which is a real advantage in a city where parking often means squeezing into a marked strip of pavement. The seat height suits most riders, and the upright riding position keeps your view above the traffic around you. For first-time riders in Vietnam, that light weight is one of the most reassuring features a rental bike can have.</p>
    <p>There is a cultural argument for the Wave as well: when you ride the same bike that millions of locals ride, you blend in. You are not the tourist on an oversized machine; you are simply another rider in the flow, which tends to make daily riding in the Old Quarter and surrounding districts feel less intimidating. If you are still building confidence before your trip, our general <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> cover helmets, fuel, parking and everyday riding habits in Hanoi.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Honda Wave rental prices in Hanoi</h2>
    <p>The verified Honda Wave rental price in Hanoi is 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week, and between 900,000 and 1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly rate is a confirmed range; contact us for the exact current monthly price for your dates and the specific bike available.</p>
    <p>These are the same numbers published on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">full rental price list</a> for the whole fleet. You can verify each rate before you book, and the price you are quoted at the shop is the price you pay for the rental period you choose. There are no separate tourist rates or seasonal surcharges hiding in the published list: what you see there is the complete set of verified prices.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">How daily, weekly and monthly rates compare</h2>
    <p>The weekly and monthly rates are much lower than stacking daily rentals. Renting a Honda Wave by the day for seven days would cost 1,050,000 VND, while the weekly rate is 700,000 VND. That is a saving of 350,000 VND in a single week, which is significant on a backpacker budget.</p>
    <p>The gap grows with time. Thirty daily rentals would cost 4,500,000 VND, while the monthly Honda Wave rate is between 900,000 and 1,200,000 VND. In other words, a month on a Wave costs roughly a quarter of what daily rentals would cost. Even the top of the monthly range is less than a fifth of the stacked daily price.</p>
    <p>A useful way to compare periods is to look at the cost per day. The daily rate is 150,000 VND. The weekly rate works out to 100,000 VND per day. The monthly rate works out to between 30,000 and 40,000 VND per day. The longer the period, the cheaper each day of riding becomes, and the difference is large enough that choosing the right period usually matters more than choosing between the two or three cheapest models. If your stay is measured in weeks rather than days, start from the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental in Hanoi</a> hub, which explains how long-term hire works and which models make sense for longer periods.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Honda Wave versus other rental bikes</h2>
    <p>The Wave sits in the cheapest rental tier, alongside the Yamaha Sirius. Both are semi-automatic bikes renting for 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND per month. The main difference between them is brand and styling rather than price; the Sirius is Yamaha's answer to the same utility brief, and the choice between the two usually comes down to which one is available and which feels better when you sit on it.</p>
    <p>If you want an automatic scooter instead of a semi-automatic, the Yamaha Mio and Honda Click rent for the same 150,000 VND per day, while the Honda Vision and Honda Air Blade rent for 200,000 VND per day. The 50cc machines and the electric motorbike also rent for 200,000 VND per day. For a full comparison of automatic models, read the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/">Hanoi scooter rental</a> hub, and for the lightest category see the <a href="/hanoi-50cc-motorbike-rental/">50cc motorbike rental</a> guide.</p>
    <p>Comparing categories is genuinely useful because the daily difference between a Wave and a Vision is only 50,000 VND, but over a month the published ranges diverge more, so the period you rent for changes which bike is the rational choice. A rider staying a month could either take the cheapest tier and save, or spend the difference on an automatic for comfort. Both decisions are defensible; what matters is that you make the comparison with real numbers before you arrive.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">What riding a semi-automatic actually feels like</h2>
    <p>A semi-automatic bike has a foot lever for gears but no hand clutch. You roll off the throttle, tap the lever with your left foot, and roll back on. The left hand does nothing except brake and steer, which surprises riders who learned on manual bikes. Most riders get comfortable with the rhythm within a day, and the Wave in particular has a forgiving gearbox that tolerates lazy shifts and low-speed maneuvers.</p>
    <p>The advantage over a fully manual bike is obvious: no clutch control at junctions, no stalling when traffic restarts. The disadvantage versus an automatic scooter is that you still shift. In stop-and-go Old Quarter traffic, some riders prefer not to think about gears at all. That is a personal preference, not a safety question, and both categories are rented to beginners every day.</p>
    <p>Braking deserves a note. Like most underbone bikes, the Wave brakes with a front disc and a rear drum, and riders in Vietnam use both brakes together smoothly rather than grabbing the front lever alone. Wet painted road markings and manhole covers become slippery in the rain, so the standard advice is to brake earlier and lighter than you would on dry Western roads. These habits apply to every bike you can rent here, not just the Wave.</p>
    <p>If you have never ridden in Vietnam, the step-by-step <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how to rent a motorbike in Hanoi</a> guide walks through pickup, inspection and the first ride, including what to check before you ride away.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Everyday running: fuel, storage and trips</h2>
    <p>The Wave is a small-capacity underbone, which means fuel costs stay modest over a normal day of city riding. Fuel stations are dense in Hanoi and along the main highways out of the city, so range anxiety is rarely a factor for day trips in the capital. For everything about fueling, station etiquette and planning fuel for longer rides, see the general guidance in our <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> hub.</p>
    <p>Storage is minimal: a small under-seat or rear rack space for a rain jacket, a lock and a backpack. The Wave is not the bike for two full-sized suitcases, but that is true of every bike in the fleet. Riders carrying more gear usually plan around the bike rather than choosing a bigger machine, because the price gap between tiers is small while the riding advantages of a light bike in Hanoi are constant.</p>
    <p>For trips beyond the city, the Wave is a credible choice on the main paved routes: the engine is built for sustained everyday riding, and the bike is light enough to manage comfortably at the speeds typical of provincial roads. Our <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub covers route planning, day trips and what to think about before riding out of the city. As with any rented bike, confirm with us how long you plan to keep it, because trip dates change the rental period that makes financial sense.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">Who should rent a Honda Wave</h2>
    <p>The Wave is the right choice when budget and simplicity matter more than comfort features. It suits riders who plan to cover real distance, because the engine is built for everyday utility rather than showroom appeal, and it suits anyone who wants the cheapest verified weekly or monthly rate in the fleet.</p>
    <p>It is less suitable for riders who want a fully automatic experience, a larger seat or more storage. An automatic scooter such as the Click or Vision is easier to operate, while the Air Blade offers a bigger body for the same daily rate as the Vision. None of these choices is unsafe; they are about comfort and budget. When in doubt, ask to see the actual bikes available for your dates before deciding, and ride the one that fits rather than the one that photographs well.</p>
    <p>The Wave also earns its place for longer stays. Renting the cheapest tier for a month keeps your total transport cost low while leaving budget for everything else in Hanoi, and for many long-stay visitors that trade is exactly what they want. Expats and remote workers staying a season can read the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly rental guide</a> for how the long-term options are structured.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">How to book a Honda Wave</h2>
    <p>Call <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a>, email <a href="mailto:nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com">nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com</a>, or visit the shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi. We are open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily, and the rental is completed at the shop. Contact us ahead of your dates so we can confirm a Honda Wave is available.</p>
    <p>Before you arrive, it helps to know which rental period you want, because the daily, weekly and monthly rates differ significantly. For questions about deposits, documents or rental conditions, ask directly and check the answers in our <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> hub before your trip.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about Honda Wave rental prices</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How much does it cost to rent a Honda Wave in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>The verified rates are 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly rate is a confirmed range, so contact the shop for the exact current monthly price for your dates.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Is the Honda Wave the cheapest motorbike rental in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>The Wave shares the cheapest published tier with the Yamaha Sirius and, among automatic scooters, the Honda Click and Yamaha Mio. All of these rent for 150,000 VND per day. The published list is the complete source of verified rates.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should I rent a Honda Wave by the day, week or month?</h2>
      <p>If you are riding for a week or longer, the weekly and monthly rates are much cheaper than daily rentals. A week of daily rentals costs 1,050,000 VND against a 700,000 VND weekly rate, so the period you choose changes the total more than the model does.</p>
    </article>

